# Reference vocabulary coverage — what each tool accepts that we do not.
#
# 212 word(s) outside our surface, across the references installed
# here. A word, not a feature: a family of dotted spellings is often one
# rule, and one line here can be a week's work or an afternoon's.
#
# Words the tool refuses on the target we assemble for are counted
# separately and are NOT in that total. A 68881 mnemonic under
# -m68000, a 6309 one in 6809 mode, `.a16` outside 65816 mode: vasm,
# lwasm and ca65 all refuse those themselves, so they measure the
# width of a wider target rather than a gap in this one.
#
# What it misses: a spelling that differs from the harvested word by a
# character the harvest does not keep. sjasmplus takes an optional
# leading `.` on every directive, and a binary storing `db` yields
# `db` — so the ~30 dotted spellings it also accepts were invisible
# here until someone read the list and noticed the rule.
#
# What it still over-counts: a word that is vocabulary somewhere other
# than statement position. rgbasm knows `af` as a register and `acos`
# as an expression function, and both are offered here as operations,
# so both read as missing. Some of those are real (we have no `acos`)
# and some are not (we do take `af`). Reading a line here is still
# cheaper than finding it, which is the point.
#
# Regenerate with `cargo xtask surface --write` (needs the reference
# tools installed; it questions each one a few thousand times, so it
# takes minutes rather than seconds).
#
# A lower bound. Only words stored in the binary as literal bytes are
# found, and names are all this measures — whether a word *means* the
# same thing is the differential corpus's question, and the expensive
# half.

## acme (This is ACME, release 0.97 ("Zem"), 28 June 2020)
# 1177 candidate(s) harvested; 74 recognised here, 2 more the tool itself
# refuses on this target; 0 of the 74 outside our surface

